#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "If we let\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac x 3\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int2\\sin (\\frac{x}{3})dx &= 2\\int\\sin u \\cdot 3du \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3\\int\\sin udu \\\\\n&=-6\\cos u + C \\\\\n&=-6\\cos (\\frac{x}{3}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "If we let\n\n$$\nu=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int5\\csc (6x + \\frac{π}{3})\\cot (6x + \\frac{π}{3})dx &= 5\\int\\csc (6x + \\frac{π}{3})\\cot (6x + \\frac{π}{3})dx \\\\\n&= 5\\int\\csc u\\cot u \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\\int\\csc u\\cot udu \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{6}\\csc u + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{6}\\csc (6x + \\frac{π}{3}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx^{2}\\sec^{2} (x^{3} - \\frac{π}{6})dx &= \\int\\sec^{2} (x^{3} - \\frac{π}{6}) \\cdot x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\sec^{2} (u) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\sec^{2} (u)du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\tan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\tan (x^{3} - \\frac{π}{6}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nSo, let\n\n$$\nu = 1+\\tan^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{\\tan x\\sec^{2} x}{1 + \\tan^{2} x}dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{1}{1 + \\tan^{2} x} \\cdot \\tan x\\sec^{2} xdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\ln 2 - \\ln 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81755e47-2beb-527e-84ed-50767fe67b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065369} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d23ab4-1675-55ae-8c22-928190fe5b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065370} {:answer/id #uuid "333eb1d7-82e8-5400-bbce-cbe660277d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065371} {:answer/id #uuid "57312e2d-e898-5775-8834-f9972fa821e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372} {:answer/id #uuid "5829fcfd-be18-5dde-9581-0270d1968bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57312e2d-e898-5775-8834-f9972fa821e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\sec (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372} :answer-field/id #uuid "60902609-13d1-586f-bdab-f6c27d5d80a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065368}] :question/id #uuid "8ab614